In a single-quoted PS string, `"` and `\` are literal, `''` escapes a single quote. The line contains single quotes around `"` char literals: `'"'` → in PS: `'"'` written as `''"''`. So:

$good = '            sb.append(''"'').append(pairs[i]).append("\":\"").append(pairs[i + 1]).append(''"'');'

That's clean. Replace the line matching 'pairs\[i\]\)\.append' entirely.

payload.arguments:
{"cmd": "$p='src\test\java\com\example\starter\DischargeApiTest.java'
$lines=[System.IO.File]::ReadAllLines($p)
$good='            sb.append(''"'').append(pairs[i]).append("\":\"").append(pairs[i + 1]).append(''"'');'
for($i=0;$i -lt $lines.Count;$i++){ if($lines[$i] -match 'pairs\[i\]\)\.append'){ $lines[$i]=$good } }
[System.IO.File]::WriteAllLines($p,$lines,(New-Object System.Text.UTF8Encoding($false)))
Select-String -Path $p -Pattern 'pairs\[i\]' | Select-Object -ExpandProperty Line"}
